s system, and a separate Side Quest. Round 2 Evaluation Criteria Problem Understanding & Relevance: How effectively the team addresses the chosen problem. Innovation & Creativity: Originality and usefulness of the solution. Technical Implementation: Quality of implementation and demonstrable progress. Feasibility & Real-World Impact: Practicality and potential impact. Execution & Completeness: Quality and completeness of the solution within the given time. Demo & Presentation: Clarity of the demonstration and explanation. Mid-Evaluation The Mid-Evaluation will take place at approximately 11:00 PM on 25 October 2026 . Mentors will assess the team's problem understanding, solution approach, technical progress, technologies used, feasibility, and implementation plan. Teams should be prepared to explain their progress and demonstrate the work completed so far. Stars — Peer Engagement System The Stars system allows teams to explore other projects and recognise promising ideas and execution. Each team will receive Star stickers marked with its own team letter. Teams can award their Stars to other teams during designated engagement windows. Self-voting is not allowed. Stars will contribute a limited portion of the overall evaluation score. The organisers will supervise and validate the Stars count. Side Quest The Side Quest is a separate challenge conducted alongside the main hackathon. It opens at 12:00 AM on 26 October 2026 and has its own winner recognition and evaluation. Further challenge instructions will be communicated by the organisers. Multiple Themes & Prize Stacking Teams may participate in multiple themes and win awards in more than one theme. Prize stacking is allowed , including winning additional awards, provided the team meets the eligibility criteria for each award. Best Women in Tech Team Award This award recognises an outstanding team consisting entirely of women . It is separate from the main theme prizes, and eligible teams can receive it alongside other awards. Rul",
